
Key Findings
Bank Overview

Executive Summary

	This analysis examined approximately 60,000 customers, their deposit accounts, payment cards, over 202,000 card transactions, and a 22,000-loan portfolio across 136 branches, providing a comprehensive assessment of customer behaviour, operational efficiency, credit risk, and fraud exposure. The customer base is well balanced, comprising 31,344 males (52.24%), 27,412 females (45.69%), and 1,244 customers (2.07%) identifying as other. Customer acquisition remained stable throughout the year, with monthly registrations ranging from 4,617 to 5,171, while transaction activity was evenly distributed across Withdrawals (40.19%), Deposits (39.84%), and Other Transactions (19.97%), reflecting consistent customer engagement and healthy liquidity. Fraud analysis identified 1,037 fraudulent transactions from 202,831 card transactions, representing a low fraud rate of 0.51%; however, these incidents resulted in more than £1.79 million in losses. Fraud activity peaked in 2023 with 150 cases and £246,652.03 in losses, while January recorded the highest monthly fraud volume (103 cases). Notably, no customer experienced more than one reported fraud incident, suggesting fraud was dispersed across the customer base rather than concentrated among repeat victims.

	The bank’s lending portfolio remains resilient, with 90.25% of total loan value classified as Active (63.98%) or Closed (26.27%), while 9.75% is either Defaulted (6.98%) or Written Off (2.77%), representing the institution’s primary credit risk exposure. Operational analysis identified 3,530 dormant accounts holding approximately ₹4.72 billion (£36.74 million) in customer funds, many of which remain linked to active payment cards, alongside 40,945 expired payment cards, highlighting opportunities to improve operational efficiency, strengthen fraud controls, and reduce servicing costs. Overall, the analysis indicates a financially stable institution with a diversified customer base, a strong lending portfolio, and a low incidence of fraud, while identifying clear opportunities to enhance credit risk management, fraud prevention, data quality, and operational governance to improve long-term performance and profitability.

Customers
	•Maharashtra has the largest customer base with 11,981 customers, accounting for 19.97% of all customers.
	•	Customer demographics remain balanced, comprising 31,344 males (52.24%), 27,412 females (45.69%), and 1,244 customers (2.07%) identifying as other.
	•	Customer acquisition is consistent throughout the year, ranging from 4,617 registrations in February to 5,171 in August—a difference of only 554 customers, indicating no meaningful seasonality.
	•	Private Salaried customers generate the highest economic value, with an average annual income of ₹1,834,832 and a combined income of ₹15.86 billion.
	•	Although Freelancers represent the largest occupational segment, they record the lowest average annual income, suggesting lower overall earning capacity despite their volume.


Accounts
	•	The analysis identified 3,530 dormant accounts holding ₹4.72 billion (£36.74 million) in customer funds.
	•	A significant proportion of these dormant accounts still have active payment cards, exposing the bank to operational inefficiencies, unnecessary card servicing costs, and potential fraud or compliance risks.
	•	Branch-level analysis highlighted the top five branches by average customer balance, providing insight into where the bank’s highest-value deposits are concentrated.


Transactions
	•	Transaction activity is highly balanced:
	•	Withdrawals: 40.19%
	•	Deposits: 39.84%
	•	Other transactions: 19.97%
	•	Withdrawals exceed deposits by only 0.35 percentage points, indicating stable customer cash flow rather than sustained outflows.
	•	Transaction records were standardised by consolidating Transfer In into Deposits and Transfer Out into Withdrawals, improving reporting accuracy and simplifying future analysis.



Cards
	•	The bank currently has 40,945 expired payment cards, indicating a sizeable inactive card portfolio.
	•	Dormant accounts with active cards present an additional operational and compliance concern, as inactive customers continue to hold valid payment instruments.


Loans
	•	The loan portfolio is valued across four key statuses:
	•	Active: 63.98%
	•	Closed: 26.27%
	•	Default: 6.98%
	•	Written Off: 2.77%
	•	Combined, Default and Written-off loans account for 9.75% of the total loan portfolio, representing the bank’s highest credit risk exposure.
	•	Average loan values are broadly consistent across all loan statuses, indicating that loan size alone is not a strong predictor of repayment performance.


Loan Risk by products
	•	Education Loans recorded the highest number of defaults, making them the bank’s highest-risk loan product.
	•	Gold Loans closely followed, indicating elevated repayment risk despite being asset-backed.
	•	Personal Loans recorded the lowest number of defaults among the analysed loan products, making them comparatively lower risk.


Business Impact
	•	Higher default concentrations in Education and Gold Loans increase credit losses, reduce profitability, and may require tighter underwriting standards or enhanced collections strategies.
	•	The relatively lower default rate for Personal Loans suggests current lending and risk assessment practices for this product are performing more effectively.


Loan risk by Banks 
The analysis of 136 branches reveals considerable variation in loan performance and credit risk. Ahmedabad Branch 2 recorded the highest default rate at 20.83%, with 5 defaults out of 24 loans, followed by Hyderabad Branch 5 (18.52%), Kolkata Branch 3 (16.67%), and Lucknow Branch 8 (16.67%). These branches represent the highest credit risk and should be prioritised for review.
In contrast, several branches, including Chandigarh Branch 3, Mumbai Branch 2, Ahmedabad Branch 8, Indore Branch 3, and Chennai Branch 8, achieved a 0% default rate, demonstrating strong lending and credit monitoring practices despite managing between 19 and 35 loans.
The analysis also shows that larger loan portfolios do not necessarily lead to higher risk. For example, Pune Branch 6 managed the largest portfolio shown (74 loans worth 28.63 million) but recorded a 13.51% default rate, lower than several branches with much smaller loan books. This indicates that loan quality is driven more by underwriting standards than portfolio size.
Write-off patterns also differed across branches. Chandigarh Branch 3 recorded the highest write-off rate (9.52%) despite having no current defaults, suggesting write-offs may be linked to older non-performing loans rather than recent lending activity.

Data Quality & Operational Risks
	•	Several loans marked as Closed have no corresponding repayment records in the loan payments table (e.g., Loan ID 183729).
	•	This inconsistency may be caused by:
	•	Missing repayment transactions.
	•	Incomplete data migration.
	•	Incorrect loan status assignments.
	•	If unresolved, these issues could affect financial reporting, regulatory compliance, portfolio performance measurement, and management decision-making.

Fraud Analysis Summary

	Analysis of 202,831 card transactions identified 1,037 fraudulent transactions, resulting in an overall fraud rate of 0.51% (approximately 1 in every 196 transactions). Despite the relatively low occurrence, fraudulent transactions resulted in over £1.79 million in total financial losses, with an average fraud value ranging between £1,600 and £1,940 per transaction.

	Fraud activity remained fairly consistent between 2018 and 2025, with annual fraud cases ranging from 102 to 150. 2023 recorded the highest fraud volume (150 cases) and the greatest financial loss (£246,652.03), while 2024 had the lowest number of fraud cases (102) among the full years analysed. Monthly analysis showed January experienced the highest fraud activity (103 cases, £191,246.73 in losses), whereas November recorded the fewest incidents (66 cases) and the lowest losses (£110,836.90). Additionally, no customer experienced more than one reported fraud incident, indicating that fraudulent activity was spread across individual customers rather than concentrated among repeat victims, suggesting opportunistic attacks rather than repeated account compromise.


Recommendation Summary
	•	Strengthen credit risk management by tightening underwriting standards and enhancing monitoring for Education and Gold Loans, which recorded the highest default rates, while replicating successful lending practices from branches with consistently low or zero default rates.
	•	Enhance fraud detection and prevention by implementing real-time transaction monitoring for high-risk merchant categories and high-value transactions. Although only 0.51% of transactions were fraudulent, losses exceeded £1.79 million, highlighting the financial impact of even low fraud volumes.
	•	Increase fraud surveillance during high-risk periods, particularly January and throughout years with elevated fraud activity such as 2023, by applying risk-based authentication and transaction limits where appropriate.
	•	Reduce operational risk by identifying dormant accounts, deactivating unnecessary active cards linked to inactive customers, and removing expired cards to minimise servicing costs and reduce potential fraud exposure.
	•	Improve data quality and governance by reconciling closed loans with missing repayment records and strengthening data validation processes to ensure accurate financial reporting, regulatory compliance, and reliable performance measurement.
	•	Adopt a bank-wide fraud prevention strategy rather than focusing solely on repeat victims, as the analysis found no customer experienced multiple reported fraud incidents, indicating fraud was dispersed across the customer base.
	•	Leverage high-value customer segments, particularly Private Salaried customers, through targeted retention, personalised product offerings, and cross-selling initiatives to increase customer lifetime value and drive sustainable revenue growth.
	•	Develop interactive risk dashboards combining loan performance, branch defaults, fraud trends, card activity, and customer behaviour to enable proactive monitoring, faster decision-making, and early identification of emerging risks.








